#---
# purpose: multi-clause dispatch and recursive fact keep their chosen clauses and continuations stable
# root: main/0
# assert.metric.semantic.activations: 3
# assert.metric.semantic.executables: 3
# assert.metric.semantic.callsites: 11
# assert.metric.call_edges.count: 11
# assert.edge: fact/1[int] | @141-152 | direct | fact/1
# assert.edge: main/0[] | @174-185 | direct | classify/1
# assert.edge: main/0[] | @232-239 | direct | fact/1
# snapshot.call_edges: call_edges
#---
def classify(0), do: :zero
def classify(n) when n > 0, do: :positive
def classify(_), do: :negative

def fact(0), do: 1
def fact(n), do: n * fact(n - 1)

def main() do
  dbg(classify(0))
  dbg(classify(7))
  dbg(classify(-3))
  dbg(fact(5))
end

# Paper answer.
#
#   classify/1[int] -> :negative | :positive | :zero
#     Three clauses, one activation: the guard chooses among them and the return is
#     the union of what the reachable clauses yield.
#   fact/1[int] -> int
#     Recursive, so it forms a component whose only member is itself.
#   main/0[] -> int
#
# Kernel.dbg/1 is the identity on its argument, so it has one activation per distinct
# argument type that reaches it. Two arrive here:
#   Kernel.dbg/1[:negative | :positive | :zero] -> :negative | :positive | :zero
#   Kernel.dbg/1[int]                           -> int
#
# Eleven call edges. An edge names the callee activation its span reaches, and the
# edge's return is that activation's return. The fourth dbg call reaches
# Kernel.dbg/1[int] and returns int. That holds whatever the three earlier dbg edges
# returned: a sibling activation of the same function answers its own question and
# never this one's.
#
# This answer sits below the code because the call_edges snapshot pins byte spans
# measured from the first line after the frontmatter, so a comment above the code
# would move every span in it.
